:: Museum Hopeless Cycle Painting
<<effects>>
<<npc Winter>><<person1>>

You bring the snake-<<worldFemale 'woman' 'man'>> painting, frame and all, to Winter. <<He>> nearly drops what <<hes>> holding, gently setting it down before taking the painting from you. "This is a centuries-old piece. I had thought it destroyed. Where did you find it?"
<br><br>

<<speechresolve>>
<<if _speechAttitudeResolved is "docile">>
	"I-It was in a maze out in the farmland, <<sir>>" you say. "V-Very scary place..."
	<br><br>
	Your hands stay clasped in front of you, as if Winter's question is another test.
<<elseif (_speechAttitudeResolved is "meek" or _speechAttitudeResolved is "shy")>>
	"It was in a maze out in the farmland," you say. "A scary place."
	<br><br>
	The memory of the maze lingers. You still expect to hear scales sliding through the grass.
<<elseif (_speechAttitudeResolved is "polite" or _speechAttitudeResolved is "flamboyant")>>
	"It was in a maze out in the farmland, <<sir>> Winter" you say. "Very old place."
	<br><br>
	You try to make the answer sound useful, though the thought of the snakes still prickles under your skin.
<<elseif (_speechAttitudeResolved is "bratty" or _speechAttitudeResolved is "cruel")>>
	"It was in a maze out in the farmland, somewhere almost as old as..." You think better about finishing that remark.
	<br><br>
	Winter's glare makes the unfinished insult feel louder than if you had said it.
<<else>>
	"It was in a maze out in the farmland," you say. "Along with lots of snakes."
	<br><br>
	You keep your voice steady. The painting feels heavier now that it is in Winter's hands.
<</if>>
<br><br>

"The temple would prefer this be used as firewood. I'll make sure it's safe here." <<He>> admires the carvings on the frame for a moment, thumbing the cut on the canvas before placing it against the wall. <<He>> returns with <<income 100000 "museumAntique">>, and hands it to you.
<<if !$museum_paintings_intro>>
	<<set $museum_paintings_intro to true>>
	"There are other paintings that I would pay well to have in my collection," <<he>> says. "I do not know where any of them could be, however. <span class="blue">If you find any, please bring them to me.</span>"
<</if>>
<br><br>

<<link [[Next|Museum]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Museum Hopeless Cycle Story
<<effects>>
<<npc Winter>><<person1>>

<<set $hcChallenge to {
	handLongPatrol: false, handStrict: false, handCruel: false,
	torchFaster: false, torchSnuffer: false, torchDarker: false,
	faithBanish: false, faithBurden: false, faithBlooms: false,
	forestFurther: false, forestNoShortcut: false, forestItem: false,
	waterLeaky: false, waterDirty: false, waterEarly: false,
	itemDirty: false, itemFind: false, itemNoHide: false,
	prisonerFood: false, prisonerHealth: false, prisonerClean: false,
	suspicionIncrease: false, suspicionDecrease: false, suspicionSermon: false,
	dogsTwice: false, dogsEarly: false, dogsDouble: false,
	timeSleep: false, timeLonger: false, timePrayer: false
}>>

<<set $hcEndings ||= []>>
<<set _list1 to $hcEndings.sort()>>
<<if localStorage.getItem("hopelessCycle") is null>>
	<<run localStorage.setItem("hopelessCycle", $hcEndings)>>
<</if>>
<<set _list2 to localStorage.getItem("hopelessCycle").split(",").sort()>>
<<set _memory to []>><<set _achieved to []>>
<<if !_list1.isEqual(_list2)>>
	<<for _i range ["F","E","D","C","B","A"]>>
		<<if !_list1.includes(_i) and _list2.includes(_i)>>
			<<set _memory.push(_i)>>
			<<set $hcEndings.pushUnique(_i)>>
		<</if>>
	<</for>>
	<<run localStorage.setItem("hopelessCycle", localStorage.getItem("hopelessCycle").split(",").concatUnique($hcEndings))>>
<</if>>
<<hopelessCycleQuickReadLinks>>
<<if _memory.length gte 1>>
	Looking at the painting, <span class="lblue">a foreign memory strikes you</span>.
	<<if $museumAntiques.paintings.paintingsnake is "talk">>
		"I think I know this one," you say. "This is <span class="gold">'Hopeless Cycle'</span>, right? The story of the witch and the gaoler."
		<br><br>

		Winter smiles. "I see you've done your research. Yes, that's exactly right. Out of curiosity, what variations of this tale have you heard? There are a number of them."
		<br><br>

		You recount the foggy memories you have of the painting's story. As you speak, and as Winter coaxes you, <span class="gold">the memories become clearer, more detailed, until you remember...</span>
	<<else>>
		"I think I remember some versions of this," you say. "Versions you never told me."
		<br><br>

		Winter's eyebrows raise. "Oh? Doing some reading in your own time, are you? Out of curiosity, what variations of this tale have you heard? Including the ones I've told."
		<br><br>

		You go through the foggy memories you have of the painting's story, comparing them to the stories you remember hearing from Winter. As you speak, and as Winter coaxes you, <span class="gold">the memories become clearer, more detailed, until you remember...</span>
	<</if>>
	<br><br>

	<<hopelessCycleEndings>>
	You finish.
	<<if $hcEndings.includesAll("F","E","D","C","B","A")>>
		Winter looks impressed. "That's quite a few of them. There's only one commonly-accepted ending left, in fact.
		<<if $museumAntiques.paintings.paintingsnake is "talk">>
			It involves a few supernatural elements I would've excluded as to not dilute the story, but it's clear you're ready for them.
		<<else>>
			It involves a few supernatural elements I've previously excluded as to not dilute the story, but it's clear you're ready for them.
		<</if>>
		It involves cross-referencing some other legends. Do you know that one?"
		<<if _list2.includes("S")>>
			<br><br>

			You open your mouth to answer, but pause. You had been so sure that you did, but now that you're searching for the memory, it seems to evade you. Winter notices your confusion. "Some stories simply insist on being told, as I understand it. If you can't remember... well, it's a good thing I'm here, isn't it?"
		<<else>>
			You don't think you do.
		<</if>>
		<br><br>

		<<He>> walks off for a moment, returning with a chair and inviting you to sit. The painting hangs on the wall next to you, the figure's weeping gaze bearing down.
		<br><br>

		"There's two that are relevant. The first is a tale of an entity in the part of the old sewers that was once the temple's disposal channel.
		<span class="gold">This version supposes that the gaoler received a gift from it after 'feeding' it many objects, especially 'tainted' ones, by dumping them into the waterway.
		Part of the legend involves throwing a lit torch into the channel to get its attention.</span>"
		<br><br>

		<<He>> looks to the painting. "The other legend comes into play when the gaoler <span class="gold">brings that gift to the lake before the day the witch was branded with crosses</span>, but I'm getting ahead of myself.
		Let's work our way to that part of the story first."
		<br><br>
	<<else>>
		Winter nods. "I see. That skips some of the stories, then. If you're willing to listen, I'm always glad to share history. Folklore or otherwise."
		<br><br>

		<<He>> walks off for a moment, returning with a chair and inviting you to sit. The painting hangs on the wall next to you, the figure's weeping gaze bearing down.
		<br><br>
	<</if>>
	<<link [[Let <<him>> continue|Museum Hopeless Cycle Story Continue]]>><<set $phase to 1>><</link>>
	<br>
	<<link [[Back out|Museum Hopeless Cycle Story Back]]>><</link>>
	<<exit>>
<<elseif $hcEndings.includes("S")>>
	<<hopelessCycleEndings>>
	"Could you tell me the story behind this painting again?" you ask Winter.
	<br><br>

	<<He>> stops what <<hes>> doing. "If you're willing to listen, I'm always glad to share history. Folklore or otherwise."
	<br><br>

	<<He>> walks off for a moment, returning with a chair and inviting you to sit. The painting hangs on the wall next to you, the figure's weeping gaze bearing down.
	<br><br>

	Winter sits down too. "The painting is titled <span class="gold">'Hopeless Cycle'</span>. It's written on the back of the frame in an older tongue, and the translation could also mean 'desperate spiral' or similar.
	It evokes the feeling of being trapped in self-fulfilling loops. It's at least three centuries old. Though you know all this by now, of course."
	<br><br>

	<<link [[Ask Winter if there are any other variations on the story|Museum Hopeless Cycle Story Challenge]]>><</link>> | <span class="blue"><i>Challenge options</i></span> <!-- Appears even with blind stats enabled. Intentional -->
	<br>
	<<link [[Let <<him>> continue|Museum Hopeless Cycle Story Continue]]>><<set $phase to 1>><</link>>
	<<exit>>
<<elseif $hcEndings.includesAll("F","E","D","C","B","A") and !$hcEndings.includes("S")>>
	<<hopelessCycleEndings>>
	"You said you'd add some missing details this time. I'd like to hear them."
	<br><br>

	Winter nods. "There's a few supernatural elements I've excluded as to not dilute the story. It involves cross-referencing some other legends."
	<br><br>

	<<He>> walks off for a moment, returning with a chair and inviting you to sit. The painting hangs on the wall next to you, the figure's weeping gaze bearing down.
	<br><br>

	"There's two that are relevant. The first is a tale of an entity in the part of the old sewers that was once the temple's disposal channel.
	<span class="gold">This version supposes that the gaoler received a gift from it after 'feeding' it many objects, especially 'tainted' ones, by dumping them into the waterway.
	Part of the legend involves throwing a lit torch into the channel to get its attention.</span>"
	<br><br>

	<<He>> looks to the painting. "The other legend comes into play when the gaoler <span class="gold">brings that gift to the lake before the day the witch was branded with crosses</span>, but I'm getting ahead of myself.
	Let's work our way to that part of the story first."
	<br><br>

	<<He>> looks back to you. "You know how this goes by now. The subject is a controversial local folk figure. This was almost certainly painted long after the event that inspired it.
	The temple has attempted, many times, to censor or revise the tale, as it's quite the embarrassment to their more modern image.
	Or so they claim. This painting represents the story of a beloved wise <<worldFemale 'woman' 'man'>> they persecuted for apostasy and witchcraft..."
<<else>>
	<<if $museumAntiques.paintings.paintingsnake is "talk">>
		"You said the temple would use this as firewood. There must be a story behind that," you say.
		<br><br>

		<<He>> nods. "It's a very interesting tale, steeped in the fog of religious persecution and myth. There's truth behind it, but it's been romanticised, disparaged, and warped over time. In other words, the story behind it is a folktale of a very real event."
	<<else>>
		<<hopelessCycleEndings>>
		"Could you tell me the story behind this painting again?" you ask Winter.
		<br><br>

		<<He>> stops what <<hes>> doing. "If you're willing to listen, I'm always glad to share history. Folklore or otherwise."
	<</if>>
	<br><br>

	<<He>> walks off for a moment, returning with a chair and inviting you to sit. The painting hangs on the wall next to you, the figure's weeping gaze bearing down.
	<br><br>

	Winter sits down too. "The painting is titled <span class="gold">'Hopeless Cycle'</span>. It's written on the back of the frame in an older tongue, and the translation could also mean 'desperate spiral' or similar.
	It evokes the feeling of being trapped in self-fulfilling loops. It's at least three centuries old."
	<br><br>

	<<He>> runs <<his>> fingers along the frame. "Both burns and water damage here. To be expected on such aged pieces, but what confounds me is this cut on the canvas. There are traces of silver within the wound."
	<br><br>

	<<He>> looks back to you. "The subject is a controversial local folk figure. This was almost certainly painted long after the event that inspired it.
	The temple has attempted, many times, to censor or revise the tale, as it's quite the embarrassment to their more modern image.
	Or so they claim. This painting represents the story of a beloved wise <<worldFemale 'woman' 'man'>> they persecuted for apostasy and witchcraft..."
<</if>>
<br><br>

<<link [[...the injustice of which immortalised <<worldFemale 'her' 'him'>> in myth.|Hopeless Cycle]]>><<pass 30>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

